uring its tenure in 450-449 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "kaeso-duilius-longus", "sourceName": "Kaeso Duilius Longus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ancient-rome", "targetName": "Ancient Rome", "context": "Kaeso Duilius Longus's political career and participation in the Decemvirate occurred within the geographical and political confines of the early Roman Republic."}, {"sourceSlug": "kaeso-duilius-longus", "sourceName": "Kaeso Duilius Longus", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "law-of-the-twelve-tables", "targetName": "Law of the Twelve Tables", "context": "As a member of the Second Decemvirate, Kaeso Duilius Longus participated in the final codification and promulgation of the Law of the Twelve Tables in 449 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Roman Republic", "role": "Center of political activity"}, {"name": "Forum Romanum, Roman Republic", "role": "Site of legislative assembly"}, {"name": "Latium, Italia", "role": "Regional context"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Kaeso Duilius Longus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "kaeso-duilius-longus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q731306", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caeso_Duillius_Longus"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Kaeso Duilius Longus's involvement in the Second Decemvirate was critical to the completion of the Law of the Twelve Tables, a foundational legal code, and his actions contributed to the political crisis that ultimately strengthened the Roman Republic's institutions.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-11T07:59:15Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27332258620", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Caeso Duillius Longus was a Roman politician, a member of the Second Decemvirate in 450 and 449 BC.", "newValue": "Kaeso Duilius Longus served as a prominent Roman politician, holding a seat within the controversial Second Decemvirate in 450 and 449 BCE. This legislative body, composed of ten men, was initially established to codify Rome's unwritten laws, a critical demand arising from the ongoing struggle between the patrician and plebeian classes. Duilius Longus's involvement placed him at the heart of a pivotal moment in the early Roman Republic, as the Decemvirs, under the dominant influence of Appius Claudius Crassus, overstepped their mandate and attempted to establish a tyrannical rule, ultimately leading to their downfall and the restoration of traditional republican governance.

The initial Decemvirate successfully produced the first ten tables of Roman law, but the Second Decemvirate, including Duilius Longus, faced widespread accusation of tyranny. They were tasked with adding two final tables to complete the Law of the Twelve Tables, which they did, but they also refused to relinquish their power at the end of their term in 449 BCE. Their actions, particularly the arbitrary execution of citizens and the infamous case of Verginia, whose father killed her to protect her from Appius Claudius's lust, ignited a plebeian secession to the Mons Sacer and the Aventine Hill, effectively paralyzing the city.

